@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_GoTo}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_GoTo @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1 tabindex="-1" >@GlobalValues.Component_Name_GoTo @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility . . . In Progress . . .</h1>

<p>
    There isn't an established W3C ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) pattern for a page navigation list like this, so the component is built from native HTML elements wherever possible:
    a <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">dialog</span>, a heading, a list of <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">button</span> elements and the HTML popover API. Relying on native elements means
    that focus, activation and naming behave in the way users and assistive technologies already expect.
</p>
<p>
    The viewer is a native <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">dialog</span> element shown as a non-modal popover. It is named by its heading through <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-labelledby</span>, and the
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">HeadingText</span> parameter sets that text. Because it is non-modal, the rest of the page isn't made inert and focus is never trapped. The viewer closes with
    <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Escape</kbd>, when focus leaves it, or when a marker is chosen. When it opens, focus is placed on the dialog itself, so a screen reader announces
    its name first and the user can then move on to the list.
</p>
<p>
    The markers are listed in a native <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">ul</span> of native <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">button</span> elements. The accessible name of each button is its number followed by the
    marker's label, for example "01 - Filters", which also gives voice control users something they can speak. The list is built each time the viewer opens, and markers that are hidden or inert are left out,
    so every button leads somewhere that can be reached.
</p>
<p>
    The trigger is a native <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">button</span>. It is visually hidden until it receives keyboard focus, using the same technique as a skip link, so it stays in the tab order at the
    point where the viewer is added to your layout.
</p>
<p>
    A marker is an empty, zero-size element with no role and no name, so there is nothing for a screen reader to find while the user reads the page. The label inside it is a popover that is hidden with
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">display: none</span> until it is needed, which also keeps it out of the accessibility tree. When a user chooses a marker, the label is shown and receives focus. It is named by
    its own text through <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-labelledby</span>, so assistive technologies announce where the user has arrived. The label closes when focus moves away or when
    <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Escape</kbd> is pressed, and <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Tab</kbd> continues from the marker's position on the page.
</p>
<p>
    The numbered badges shown on the page while the viewer is open are a visual aid for sighted keyboard users. They are generated with CSS and given empty alternative text, so browsers that support this
    don't announce them separately, as the number is already part of each button's name.
</p>

<h2 id="goto-keyboard">Keyboard interaction</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="goto-keyboard" class="keyboard-list">
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Ctrl + Shift + Y</kbd> - opens the viewer from anywhere on the page and places focus on it. It has no effect while a modal dialog is open.
    </li>
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Tab</kbd> - places focus on the trigger button, which becomes visible while it has focus. Inside the viewer, it moves through the list buttons and then the close button.
        Tabbing out of the viewer closes it, and focus continues to the next element after the one that had focus before the viewer opened.
    </li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Shift + Tab</kbd> reverses tab direction, including leaving the viewer backwards.</li>
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Space or Enter</kbd> - on the trigger button, opens the viewer. On a list button, closes the viewer, moves to the marker and shows its label.
    </li>
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Down Arrow</kbd> - <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Up Arrow</kbd> - moves to the next or previous list button, stopping at the first and last.
    </li>
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Home</kbd> - <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">End</kbd> - moves to the first or last list button.
    </li>
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Escape</kbd> - closes the viewer and returns focus to where it was before the viewer opened. When a marker's label is showing, it closes the label.
    </li>
</ul>

<h2 id="device-testing">Accessibility device testing</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="device-testing">
    <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows were each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on macOS was paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on iPhone was paired with mobile Safari.</li>
    <li>Talkback on Android was paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    <li>Voice access on Windows 11</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Testing was performed with the latest browser and assistive technologies versions that were available at the time of testing (see release history on the installation page for approximate testing dates)
</p>
